            <ArticleTitle>Hybrid origin of Asian aspermic Fasciola flukes is confirmed by analyzing two single-copy genes, pepck and pold.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1292/jvms.17-0406</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Nuclear gene markers, phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (pepck) and DNA polymerase delta (pold), have been developed for precise discrimination of Fasciola flukes instead of internal transcribed spacer 1. In this study, these two genes of 730 Fasciola flukes from eight Asian countries were analyzed. The results were compared with their mitochondrial NADH dehydrogenase subunit 1 (nad1) lineages for obtaining a definitive evidence of the hybrid origin of aspermic Fasciola flukes. All the flukes categorized into the aspermic nad1 lineages possessed both the fragment patterns of F. hepatica and F. gigantica (mixed types) in pepck and/or pold. These findings provide clear evidence for the hybrid origin of aspermic Fasciola lineages and suggest that &quot;aspermic Fasciola flukes&quot; should hereafter be called &quot;hybrid Fasciola flukes&quot;.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
